const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "bn:0": [
    "On the boat, Putri's mum said 'Selamat' to a neighbour. She pointed at a rock in the river. People call it Jong Batu, and it sits quietly in the middle of the water. Putri wore her baju kurung, and her tudung sat neatly. She had no flower in her hair. She heard the name Nakhoda Manis like someone opening a tale. Her grandmother said a son had a fine boat. That son pretended not to know his poor mother. It was a story to stop in time, not to frighten anyone. Putri leaned against her mum and kept her eyes on the rock.",
    "In the gentle tale her grandmother chose, the shame came early. It came before any disaster. The son felt his face burn for having pretended. He turned the boat round and came back to the bank. He said sorry in a low voice. His mother accepted his return without a cruel speech. Nobody fell into the water. No one was punished for ever, and nobody was lost in the dark. There was only an apology, and two people face to face again. The rock stayed in the river, not as a person turned to stone. It stayed as a reminder not to pretend.",
    "Her grandmother said children look at the rock and remember to say hello. They say hello even when someone's clothes are plain. Our clothes might look better, but that changes nothing. Putri whispered that pretending not to see is a lie made with the eyes. The boat went on calmly, and the rock stayed behind. It was whole, and it did its work of memory. Nobody threw anything into the water to mark it more. Putri straightened her tudung and waved at the bank. A woman there was washing a cloth.",
    "Later, at the market, a classmate in a patched dress called out to Putri. She almost walked on, because she was in a hurry. She was also ashamed of something that was not hers. Then she remembered the son in the tale. She stopped and took two steps back. She answered to her name with a clear smile. They talked for a moment about bread, as if the patch were not a wall. Her mum had seen it all and made no fuss. She only squeezed Putri's hand. Putri's baju kurung was no prettier for it, but her day was.",
    "If you ever want to pretend you cannot see someone, turn back in time. Answer with your true name. Putri remembered Nakhoda Manis and the rock called Jong Batu. It stays in the river so nobody repeats the pretending. At the market, she did not walk on. You can feel ashamed and still turn that shame into a step back. Saying sorry early keeps the story short and good. The bank is still waiting for a wave, not for silence.",
  ],
  "bn:1": [
    "Her grandmother told of the pelanduk, the small mouse deer. One day it met the tiger at the edge of the village. The tiger already had its paws on the road to the houses. There was no fight and no panicked running. The pelanduk spoke with cleverness. It knew the pride of big folk. It said the village was protected by an old agreement. It said the tiger would be admired more for leaving in peace. That was better than staying to prove its strength. The tiger, curious and vain, listened to the end. Putri, in her baju kurung and tudung, laughed quietly. The cleverness had no claws.",
    "In the tale, the pelanduk did not humiliate the tiger in front of a crowd. It did not give it an ugly name for others to repeat. It offered a way out that left the big animal whole. The village stayed calm too. The tiger accepted, because the sentence was clever and not cruel. It went away without taking anything. It left no new fear at the doors. Her grandmother pointed out that good cleverness opens a path. It makes no stage of shame. Putri nodded, thinking about school the next day.",
    "At school, a classmate bragged about having the biggest pencil case. It was the fullest, the only one that mattered. He banged it on the desk so the row would look. Putri did not laugh at him. She did not invent a fault to make him smaller in front of everyone. She said, calmly, 'Your pencil case is big, and so is the desk.' Then she added, 'Put it to one side and let us write.' There was a short silence, then a small laugh. There was no cruel audience. He put the case down, still proud, but no longer covering the books.",
    "Putri did not tell the tiger story out loud to shame him further. She did not repeat her sentence at breaktime like a trophy. She straightened her tudung and lent a rubber to someone who needed one. Then she went on with the lesson. Later the classmate showed his case without banging it. She praised one pen, only one, so the talking could carry on. At home, her grandmother heard the summary. She said the pelanduk would have recognised that size of sentence. Putri ate a piece of fruit and added no decoration to the win.",
    "If you hear someone boasting, use a clever sentence that opens space. Do not humiliate them too much. Putri remembered the pelanduk, who sent the tiger away in peace. There was no violence and no show of shame. You can be clever and still leave the other person whole. There is room at the desk once pride steps to one side. Good cleverness does not need an audience laughing.",
  ],
  "bn:2": [
    "In Kampong Ayer the houses stand on the water. The paths are boats, walkways and greetings between verandas. Putri, in her baju kurung and tudung, stood on her grandmother's veranda. She had a fruit peel in her hand. The river ran right below, far too easy to reach. Her mum had said those houses belong to all the paths. She also said the water is not a hidden basket. A cousin on the walkway waved. A neighbour's boat went past carrying bread. Putri felt the peel asking for the lazy movement of letting go.",
    "She stretched her arm over the railing. The peel stopped a hand's width above the green water. Then she remembered that the neighbour's path was that river. The bread passed there and did not deserve a peel on its bow. She pulled back and opened the little basket on the veranda. She put the peel inside, with the other things that did not belong to the river. Her mum saw and made no long speech. She only said, 'That way the boat passes clean.' Putri wiped her hand on a cloth and straightened her tudung. The water wind had lifted it.",
    "In the afternoon she helped her grandmother sweep the veranda into one corner. The rubbish went in a bag to the agreed place. It did not go over the railing when nobody was looking. She saw a bottle floating far off and told her mum. Her mum fished it out with a stick, without telling Putri to lean over. The bread neighbour came past again and waved once more. This time the river had no peel of theirs in it. Putri waved back, glad the water was still a path. Her baju kurung dried in the kitchen steam and felt light.",
    "At night the lights of the houses were reflected in the water. Putri understood one thing about that mirror. Throwing rubbish there meant throwing it on the whole village. Next day she told her cousin the veranda rule. She did not turn it into an angry sermon. Her cousin had a piece of paper in her hand and put it in the basket. She laughed at how easy it was. The two of them watched the boats until her mum called them in. Kampong Ayer went on with its wet and clean paths, clean enough for a wave.",
    "If you are in a house on the water, do not throw rubbish off the veranda. The river only seems to take everything away. Putri, in Kampong Ayer, put her peel in the basket. The boat paths belong to all the houses. The river is not a hidden hole. You can have your hand full of something useless and still look for the right place. The water between the verandas is a street. You do not sweep a street into other people's boats.",
  ],
  "bn:3": [
    "When the net came back to the jetty, the first fish shone more than the others. It was silver and whole, and Putri wanted it at once for her own plate. She wore her baju kurung, with her tudung tied down against the river wind. Her hands were already out. Her uncle had said the first fish in the net is shared. Her grandmother was right there, cleaning a cloth. The other fish were good, but they lacked that first-arrival shine. Putri felt the wish pull one way and the rule pull the other.",
    "She looked at her grandmother, who asked for nothing. Her grandmother carried on folding the cloth, as if the fish were not a test. Putri breathed and lifted the bigger fish carefully. She held it so it would not slip, and offered it with both hands. 'This is for you,' she said. Her voice came out firmer than her wish to keep it. Her grandmother smiled and took it. She did not turn the gesture into a fuss that would shame the girl. Her uncle nodded and put the other fish in the shared basket, without hiding any.",
    "At home her grandmother cooked the big fish and shared it out. Putri ate some too, so the offering did not become a loss. The taste was the same on every plate. That made Putri laugh, because the shine of the net had fitted on the whole table. Nobody took the credit away from her gesture. Nobody told her to go without a taste. Her tudung hung by the door, drying in the wind. Her baju kurung smelled slightly of river and kitchen, two honest things.",
    "Next day Putri told her classmate that choosing the biggest had been her first idea. The second idea was the one that stayed. Her classmate had an orange and, without a speech about it, split it in two. The two of them laughed at how easily a story about fish turned into fruit. Putri did not boast as if she had been perfect from the start. She told the small truth, the only one the jetty would recognise.",
    "If you want the biggest one at once, look first at who could receive it. Then offer it with both hands. Putri gave her grandmother the first fish from the net, the brightest one. The table then gave a piece back to every plate without erasing the gesture. You can feel the wish to choose for yourself and still let the best go first. Sharing is not standing outside: it puts the shine in the middle. The shared basket knows more than the hurried plate.",
  ],
  "bn:4": [
    "On the boat, the wind caught Putri's tudung and lifted it like a wing. She held it with both hands, laughing and getting in her own way. Her mum showed her how to tie it. There was a calm knot under the chin, not a hard tug. The cloth then did its work without covering her eyes. Putri repeated the movement on her own. She kept doing it until the wind passed without taking anything. Her baju kurung ballooned too, and she smoothed it down with her palm. The river went on, and the boat asked for no hurry.",
    "Further along, another girl had a scarf blowing loose. It was beating against her face. A boy on the walkway was already opening his mouth to laugh. Putri felt the same wish, brief, because the cloth looked like a clumsy flag. Then she remembered her own tudung a few minutes before. She closed her mouth and did not join in. When the boat came closer she said only, 'The wind took mine too.' Then she added, 'And my mum showed me the knot.' The girl thanked her with her eyes and tried it, still crooked, but with no audience.",
    "The boy looked embarrassed and stared at the water. Putri did not use the chance to shame him with a cleverer sentence than needed. She helped the girl find the right side of the cloth, slowly. Her mum had done the same with her. The knot was good enough, not perfect. That was enough to stop the scarf hitting her mouth. The two of them laughed together, now at the wind and not at each other. The river carried the rest of the hurry away.",
    "At home Putri practised the knot again in front of the small mirror. She did not do it to look vain. She did it so she would not need a hand over her face with every gust. Her mum saw and praised her patience. She also praised the silence on the boat, which had been harder than the knot. Putri hung her tudung on the right hook, smooth, ready for the next day. She did not tell the story as a joke about the scarf girl. She told it as something the wind teaches two people at once.",
    "If you see a scarf caught by the wind, remember your own. Do not laugh at someone still learning the knot. Putri let her mum show her how to tie the tudung. On the river, she made no fun of another girl's scarf. You can find a runaway cloth funny and still turn that fun into help. The wind belongs to everyone. Respect fits in a small boat too.",
  ],
  "kp:0": [
    "In the pine village, Min's mum said 'Annyeong' to a neighbour. Then she began the story of the green frog. The frog only ever did the opposite of what he was asked. Min wore her pink and white hanbok with a flower in her hair. She was already laughing before the end. The frog was stubborn in a comic way, not a wicked one. If he was told to go left, he jumped right, very serious. He looked as if right itself were a discovery. In the tale, his mother was only waiting for a basket. The frog thought disobeying was his art.",
    "One day his mother asked him to bring a basket from the small market. She meant the right-handled one for the apples. The frog, wanting to do the opposite, filled his arms with round stones. He set off home, very proud of the idea. Halfway there he looked at the stones and then at the pines. This time the opposite would be no use at all in the kitchen. He stopped, laughed at himself and went back to the market. He chose the basket and put one apple in it. He did not want to come back empty-handed.",
    "His mother was pleased, not because he had obeyed out of fear. She was pleased because he had thought for himself. He had done the right thing on purpose. The frog still jumped on to the wrong bench, just so as not to lose the habit at once. Then he sat in the right place, laughing. There was no angry lesson and no sad ending. There was a basket on the table. The stones stayed on the path, where stones already lived. Min clapped quietly. The flower in her hair moved, as if it wanted to jump the wrong way too.",
    "That Saturday, Min's mum asked for a basket at the small market. Min, playing at being the frog, nearly brought back a handful of pretty pebbles. She looked at the pebbles, then at her mum among the stalls. She thought as the frog had thought. She put the stones on the ground and chose the right-handled basket. Then she carried it with both hands. Her mum laughed at the face of someone who had nearly done the opposite. They bought apples, and the basket was useful.",
    "If you feel like doing the opposite just for fun, think first. Does the fun still help when someone is waiting for a basket? Min, in the pine village, nearly brought stones from the small market. Then she carried what her mum had asked for. She was like the frog who finally got it right on purpose. You can be funny and still choose the right thing. That is true when the joke no longer helps. The opposite is a game. The table, sometimes, really needs the basket.",
  ],
  "kp:1": [
    "Beside the wide river, her grandmother told of a baby who would not stop crying. Someone said the words 'dried persimmon' out loud. They said it just because it was a strange thing to say. The baby went quiet with surprise, eyes round. A tiger passing through the forest heard it. It became sure the words were the name of something huge. It ran off without touching anyone. It thought the dried persimmon was chasing it. Min, in her pink and white hanbok, laughed until the flower slipped to her ear. Her grandmother laughed too, and the river stayed wide and calm.",
    "In the tale, the tiger told the other animals that it had escaped from a word. It told them very seriously. The animals did not know whether to comfort him or to smile. Nobody was hurt and nobody was chased. The dried persimmon became a forest joke, not a threat. Her grandmother explained where the fright came from. It came only from the tiger's own imagination. Min repeated the word. Her little cousin, playing nearby, looked up. She saw the glint of an unkind idea. She could use the story to give him a real fright.",
    "Min almost opened her mouth in a deep voice. Her cousin would think the tiger was on the pine path. Then she saw his eyes, small and ready to believe. She closed the joke before it became fear. She sat beside him and told the story again. This time she laughed in the right places. She explained that the tiger was only afraid of a silly word. Her cousin laughed too. He asked to say 'dried persimmon' with his tongue tripping over it. There were no tears and no running home.",
    "Her grandmother praised the way she told it, not the near-fright. She gave each of them a slice of real persimmon. It was sweet and soft, not dried and not frightening at all. They ate by the river, watching the water carry leaves away. Min promised herself one thing about comic stories. She would not turn one into a tool for making someone younger feel small. Her cousin kept the word to tell his mum as a joke. Min warned him, 'Say it seriously only inside a laugh.' He nodded, with no fear left.",
    "If you know a story that makes people laugh because of a silly fright, be careful. Do not use it to really scare someone younger. Min laughed at the tiger and the dried persimmon. Then she told the tale to her cousin as a joke. She did not leave him scared on the pine path. You can enjoy a strange word and still choose laughter instead of fright. The tiger ran off without hurting anyone. The sweet fruit, that one, stays in the hand and chases nobody.",
  ],
  "kp:2": [
    "Her grandmother told of a brother and sister who climbed a strong rope up to the sky. They went to be safe, far from the forest. Up there they became the sun and the moon. They did not do it to escape the world. They wanted to light the pine village by day and by night. The tiger, following behind, slipped on the rope and went back to the forest. It caught nobody and held no grudge at the doors. Min listened in her pink and white hanbok, with the flower in her hair. She looked at her little brother's porch. The story was about light, not about chasing.",
    "In the tale, the village came to know the sun and the moon as those two. They worked high up and in peace. People waved at the sky without fear. The tiger stayed a forest animal, living in its own place. The rope was never a path of fright again. Her grandmother said that climbing up was a way of caring for the village. It cared for the whole village with brightness. Min liked the idea of a light born from two people helping each other. She did not ask for a version where someone gets caught.",
    "Min's little brother was on the porch, in front of a real step. It was high for his legs, and he could not get up alone. He was sulking about it. Min remembered the rope in the tale. She did not invent a dangerous game of lifting him by his coat. She gave him her hand, firm, and let him put one foot on the step. He moved slowly, with his other foot still on the safe ground. 'Now the other one,' she said, and he climbed, laughing, without letting go of her fingers. Min's flower brushed his face as she bent down.",
    "The two of them stayed on the porch for a moment. They looked at the pines and a little of the wide river below. Then they came down the same way, hand in hand, without jumping. Her brother wanted to do it again, and Min let him, always holding his hand. They went one step at a time. Their mum saw from the door and did not need to run. That night Min pointed at the moon. She said that in the tale a sister lit the village. But the step at home is climbed with a real hand. The boy nodded, sleepy, with no fear of the dark pines.",
    "If you see a little brother in front of a high step, give him your hand. Do not pull him up in a risky game. Min helped hers climb the porch. In the tale the brother and sister became the sun and the moon to light the village. The tiger went back to the forest without hurting anyone. You can want to be strong and still choose the safe step. The light in the tale stays in the sky. The held hand stays here, on the real step.",
  ],
  "kp:3": [
    "At the small market, among the baskets, her grandmother told of the two gourds. The tale was an old one from the village. The kind brother shared what he had. His gourd filled the house with rice and cloth. That was enough to live and enough to give at the door. The greedy brother wanted the same plenty for himself alone. His house filled up to the windows, so full that there was no room for a chair. There was no room for a step either. There was no fight: there was a comic and true squeeze. Min imagined not being able to reach the door.",
    "In the tale, the greedy brother looked at the cramped house. He understood that too much was not a party, it was a wall. He gave the surplus back to his neighbours, basket after basket. At last he could see the floor again and open the window. The kind brother's gourd still filled the table and fed anyone who knocked. It never blocked up the room. Her grandmother said that sharing is what gives things the right size. Min saw a pile of tangerines shining on the next stall. Her hand wanted the whole pile.",
    "Her mum bought a small bag, not the whole stall. Min held it against her hanbok as if it were the greedy gourd. Her friend arrived with no fruit at all. She looked politely at the orange without asking. Min remembered the house so full that the door would not open. She laughed at herself. She opened the bag and gave three tangerines, keeping three others. The smell stayed on both her hands. Her friend said thank you and peeled the first one right there, offering a segment back.",
    "They ate on the way to the pines without hiding the bag. Min did not go back to the stall to ask for more. She did not want to keep the big part. At home she put two tangerines in her little brother's bowl and two in her grandmother's. Her own share was still sweet. The house was not cramped with peel. She gathered it in a paper and took it to the basket. Her mum saw the sharing and did not need to tell a second tale. The hanbok had a drop of juice, and Min wiped it laughing.",
    "If you have a good bag and see someone with empty hands, share it. Do this before the house, or the pocket, gets too tight. Min gave tangerines, thinking of the two gourds. The kind brother's filled the house to the right size. The other gave back the surplus when not a step would fit. You can like a whole pile and still leave the door able to open. What is shared stays sweet. What is heaped without measure covers the window.",
  ],
  "kp:4": [
    "A magpie landed on the village fence in the pine village. It had a small bell in its beak. The bell had fallen from a house door and lain in the earth. Instead of carrying it to its nest as a decoration, the bird tapped it lightly on the wooden door. It tapped until the owner came out and recognised the sound. The magpie left the bell on the step and flew away. The house had its metal voice again. Min thought the magpie more polite than many people in a hurry. Her grandmother said giving things back is a craft of birds too.",
    "That morning Min had lost a button from her hanbok. It was white as the skirt's band. She had already decided it was gone among the pine needles. She looked for a while without blaming anyone. Then she went to meet her friend at the small market. Her house for the button was still empty. She did not accuse her friend and did not wear the face of someone who suspects. She told the magpie story, laughing. Her friend became thoughtful, touching the pocket of her coat. Below the street the wide river carried leaves away.",
    "Her friend opened her hand and showed the white button. She had found it on the path. She kept it so it would not be lost in the earth. She had not known whose it was. 'I was going to put it in the box of things without owners,' she said. 'But your hanbok has a hole the size of it.' Min felt a great relief and did not snatch the button from her hand. She waited until it was placed on her palm, as the magpie had left the bell. The stitch came out crooked and firm, and that was enough.",
    "Min did not say that her friend should have guessed sooner. She did not make the giving back into a debt. She offered her an apple from the basket. They listened to a real bell on a door far off. Perhaps it was the one from the tale, perhaps another. The magpie passed the fence again with an empty beak. Min waved without calling it to her hand. The flower in her hair was where it should be. So was the button, and the hanbok closed at the front again, pink and white.",
    "If you find something dropped that is not yours, take it back to the door. Do not keep it as a decoration. Min's friend gave the button back. The magpie in the tale took the small bell back to the step of the one who lost it. You can think a found object beautiful and still look for the hole it came from. Giving back is a simple craft. A house is more itself when its voice, or its button, returns.",
  ],
  "ye:0": [
    "On Socotra, Amat stopped in front of a tree with a strange crown. It holds a red sap used as ink and as an old medicine. She wore the colourful Yemeni sitara, with a flower in her hair. Her grandmother said 'Marhaba' to a shepherd before she spoke of the tree. The sap was not a wound: it was the tree's way of offering colour. Nobody in the family cut it to make it hurry. Amat wanted a piece of bark to paint a boat in her notebook. Her hand was already at the trunk when her grandmother covered it with her palm.",
    "'The tree stays whole,' said her grandmother. She took from the basket a small jar of ink already made. It was red and calm, closed with a cork. Amat held the jar with both hands. She did not ask again for bark, not a scratch, not a drop taken just then. She painted the boat in her notebook, sitting in the shade. The colour came out like the sap, and the tree lost nothing. The flower in her hair touched the paper. Far off, the shepherd carried no tool against the trunk, only a staff for walking.",
    "When the page was dry, they went to greet the tree. They greeted it as you greet a neighbour, without a hug that breaks off branches. Amat only leaned in with her eyes, not her nails. She saw the bark whole, working in peace in the heat. Her grandmother put the jar away again. That meant there would be ink the next day without a cutting visit. On the way, Amat showed the painted boat. She did not say she had brought a piece of the island in her pocket. She had not. Socotra kept its tree in its place.",
    "At home the ink waited on the shelf. Amat painted the flower from her hair too, crooked and cheerful, beside the boat. She told her cousin that the red sap makes colour. She also told him it makes an old medicine. She told him the tree is not opened like a jar. Her cousin wanted to go and see. He heard the rule before he put on his sandals: look, do not cut. Amat felt like a keeper of the shade, not the owner of the bark. The colourful sitara got a drop of ink on its sleeve, and she laughed.",
    "If you see a tree with red sap, do not take its bark to paint with. Ask for ink already made and leave the trunk whole. Amat, on Socotra, kept the tree safe and used her grandmother's little jar. The colour of the boat stayed on the page without a wound in the trunk. You can want the colour and still let the tree do its work. The sap is not a game for fingernails. The island stays beautiful when nobody opens what is still closed.",
  ],
  "ye:1": [
    "In Sana, among the tall earth houses with coloured windows, her grandmother told of the Queen of Sheba. She travelled, asked fair questions and shared the water from her flask. There was listening too. A sip was offered to anyone with a throat dry from the road. Amat, in her colourful sitara, found the queen more interesting for her questions than for her suitcases. Her grandmother copied the gesture of passing the flask, always to others first. Amat looked at the plate of dates. One was left, the brightest.",
    "The last date seemed to have her name written in its shine. Amat was about to say 'it's mine', her hand already open. Then she remembered the queen, who asked instead of demanding. Amat asked who had not eaten yet. She looked at her grandmother and at her cousin, just in from the street. Her cousin lowered his eyes politely. Her grandmother smiled, because the question was the whole answer. Amat put aside the idea of keeping the date. She held it out to her cousin, and he broke it in half.",
    "The two of them ate the halves. Her grandmother kept the taste of having eaten hers before. She did not stay outside the conversation either. Amat asked another question, now about the coloured glass windows. Her grandmother answered calmly, glad to be heard. The queen in the tale, she said, travelled to learn. She did not travel to carry off all the water of a land. Amat filled a glass and took it to the neighbour on the landing, without being asked. The flower in her hair caught the sun from the stairs.",
    "The neighbour said 'Marhaba' in thanks and drank slowly. Amat went back without expecting a present in return. In the courtyard her cousin kept the date stone in a paper. It would not be left on the floor of the tall house. Amat did not boast about her question. It was not a trick of queens. She only said she had nearly demanded. The right sentence had come before her hand. Her grandmother put a hand to her cheek, and the coloured windows went on painting the floor.",
    "If you want the last date, ask a question before you demand it. Listen to the answer to the end. Amat, in Sana, remembered the queen who shared water and asked fairly. She gave the date away instead of closing her hand on it. You can be thirsty for something sweet and still pass the glass and the fruit. A question opens the table better than an order. The tall house feels cooler when the flask goes from hand to hand.",
  ],
  "ye:2": [
    "In Mocha, a shepherd saw his goats happy after eating some berries from a bush. They jumped about as if the day had grown brighter. In the tale her grandmother told, the village learned to make a hot drink from those berries. Instead of keeping all of it at home, the people began to offer it around. Amat, in her colourful sitara with a flower in her hair, heard the story with a cup in her hand. It was steaming and sweet. The neighbour next door had not yet had hers. The steam rose, and so did the wish to drink it all right there.",
    "Amat took one sip, just enough to know the taste. She looked at the full cup left on the tray. Her grandmother did not say 'take it'. She waited, as the village in the tale had waited. The goats' joy was a reason to share, not a secret. Amat put the cup on a saucer. She was careful not to spill on the short way to the next door. The flower knocked against her forehead as she bent to put on her sandal. The shepherd in the tale would also have held out his hand, she imagined.",
    "The neighbour opened the door to the smell and said 'Marhaba'. Amat held out the cup with both hands. The two of them drank in the shade of the entrance, one sip each. The drink arrived without staying locked in one kitchen. Amat told her part of the tale about the happy goats. She did not enlarge it. The neighbour laughed at the goats as if she could see them in the alley. Nobody hid the tray. When they got back, her grandmother had already filled another small cup for whoever might pass.",
    "Amat did not ask for the cup back full. She did not count what she had given. She washed the saucer and left it draining, light. In the alley a real goat passed without hurry. Amat did not throw it any berry as a joke. She let it go on. The colourful sitara caught the wind of Mocha, which smelled of something warm and of an old port. The flower was still in her hair, and the afternoon fitted into several cups, not one.",
    "If you have a good hot drink, do not keep all of it. Take a cup to whoever is next door. Amat, in Mocha, remembered the happy goats and the village that learned to share. The steam went from house to house. You can enjoy the first sip and still let the second cross the alley. What is warm is better when it is not a secret. One more cup on the tray is already an invitation.",
  ],
  "ye:3": [
    "In the mountains near Sana, the marked trail could be seen by the pale stones piled up. An ibex appeared high above, slender and watchful. It seemed to show the way without coming down to the people. Amat, in her sitara with a flower in her hair, wanted a shortcut down a loose slope. It looked shorter to the eye and was more treacherous to the feet. Her grandfather held her sleeve gently. 'In this tale the ibex shows the way and nobody chases it,' he said. 'We do as it does: we follow the trail, not the hurry.'",
    "Amat looked at the shortcut, where the earth slipped in grains. She imagined a twisted step and an angry ankle. She did not need to try it. She turned her feet towards the pale stones and walked behind her grandfather, at his pace. The ibex stayed high up, whole. Nobody went after it to see it close or to bring it down. Amat's flower clung to her hair with a pin. She did not go after it when a petal fell. The trail smelled of warm stone and dry plants.",
    "Further on, her grandfather showed a bend in the path. The shortcut joined it again there. It arrived later and more tired, and Amat understood that the hurry would have been a circle. They drank water from the flask, one sip each, and saved the rest for the way down. The ibex could no longer be seen, and that was good. It had done the work of appearing and reminding them of the way. Amat did not ask to go looking for it among the rocks. She said the animal's name quietly, like a thank you.",
    "In the village she told her grandmother that the ibex was a sign and not a prize. She said the marked trail had been more interesting than the shortcut. Her grandmother served bread and did not ask about the mountain. She knew they had brought nothing back. The sitara had dust on its hem. Amat shook it out at the door, leaving the mountain on the mountain. Her grandfather hung up the flask. The day was long enough without a run down the loose slope.",
    "If you see a tempting shortcut off the marked trail, stay on the pale stones. Leave the ibex in peace up high. Amat, near Sana, did not chase it and did not cut the path. The way took them home whole. You can want to arrive first and still choose the step already agreed. The animal in the tale shows the direction and does not ask to be followed closely. The mountain likes feet that come back.",
  ],
  "ye:4": [
    "In the highlands the rain came with the smell of wet stone. The people of the house began a quiet song, of the kind sung when the water returns. Amat, in her colourful sitara with a flower in her hair, wanted to run down to the stream. It was already full and noisy between the stones. Her mother held her hand on the covered step, where the tiles sang. She told her they sang from there. The stream did not need her feet to be happy. Amat stayed, her body still leaning towards the street.",
    "The song was simple, with few words. Amat learned the chorus on the second time, and she did not sing louder than the rain. The covered step was a dry and real place. The stream below did its work of carrying the water. Amat did not pull free to go 'just to the edge'. She tapped the beat on the wood with two fingers. Her mum smiled. The flower in her hair caught a drop the wind brought, and that came as a visit from the rain. She did not dive.",
    "When the song ended a round, they started another. A neighbour on his own step answered with the same phrase, from house to house. Amat waved without leaving the shelter of the tiles. The stream grew full and then calmed a little. Nobody had to go and fetch anything for the celebration to be complete. Her mum gave her a small towel for her hair. Amat had not fallen in the water, but the air was damp. The sitara stayed colourful and dry, and the rain outside was welcome.",
    "Later, with the sky opening, they went to the stream together, slowly. They went when the current had gone down enough to see the stones. Amat did not go in. She looked, hummed the chorus again and went back to the step. The step was no longer dripping. She told her grandmother that the song had been the outing. She said the run would have been a wet mistake, and her grandmother nodded. The flower, a little tired, went into a glass, and the house smelled of good rain and bread.",
    "If you hear the rain and want to run to the full stream, wait. Stay on the covered step and sing until the water goes down. Amat learned the rain song without putting her feet in the current. Her mother stayed beside her under the tiles. You can love the sound of water and still let it pass without you. There are celebrations made dry, with the voice. The stream goes on its way, and the song fits on a step.",
  ],
  "ps:0": [
    "In her grandmother's courtyard in Bethlehem, the family olive tree spread its branches over the small well. Nearly all the olives were still green. Noor wore the Palestinian thobe with tatreez across the front, and a flower in her hair. Her grandmother said 'Marhaba' to a neighbour. 'This tree belongs to the family,' she said. 'We pick only what I point to, when it is ripe.' Noor had a stick in her hand from the morning's game. She was already picturing a shower of olives from a good thump. Her grandmother looked at the stick and then at the tree.",
    "Noor put the stick against the wall, away from the trunk. She waited for her grandmother to choose. There were three dark olives, almost black, on a low tip. Her grandmother touched them with one finger and said those were the ones. Noor picked them one by one, with a cupped hand. She did not shake the branch and did not pull at the green ones beside them. The flower in her hair brushed a leaf. She said sorry to the tree in a low voice, laughing at herself. The tatreez on the thobe rose and fell as she straightened up.",
    "The three olives went into a bowl, not into a game. Her grandmother showed her how to wash them slowly. Noor did not pick the stick up again. She did not ask to 'just tap it lightly' to hear the sound. She sat in the shade of the same tree and ate bread. She watched the green ones that had stayed in place. As she said goodbye, the neighbour took no branches: she took a wave. The olive tree stayed the family's, whole. It still had work to do on the pale olives.",
    "In the afternoon Noor drew the tree in her notebook, with only three dark dots. She showed her cousin without saying she had picked a bucketful, because she had not. Her cousin wanted to go into the courtyard. She heard the rule before reaching out: her grandmother points, the hand picks, the stick stays on the wall. The two of them watered the foot of the tree with a small bucket. The water went on the earth and not on the leaves. At last Noor's flower fell, and she left it at the base of the trunk.",
    "If you stand before a family olive tree, do not beat it for fun. Pick only the ripe olives that you are shown. Noor, in Bethlehem, left the stick on the wall and picked three dark fruits. The tree kept the rest of its time. You can want to shake a branch and still wait for the right colour. Your grandmother's tree is not a drum. It is a tall house of leaves, and it needs calm too.",
  ],
  "ps:1": [
    "Her grandmother told of a clever goat. It would not enter a house where they did not share water. It waited at the door, patiently, until it saw a bowl on the ground. Noor wore her thobe with tatreez and a flower in her hair. She thought the goat fairer than a lot of hurry. Just then the neighbour's goat put its nose through the courtyard gate. It was heading for the shade. Noor was going to close the gate, hand already on the latch. The goat was not family, and the plants were. The goat looked at the empty bowl by the well and did not come forward.",
    "Noor remembered the tale and stopped the latch halfway. The goat was not asking for the whole garden. It was asking for what the story said a house should offer. A house that wants visits on four legs should offer it. Noor went to the well and filled the bowl. She put it outside the flowerbed, where hooves would not crush the leaves. 'You can drink,' she said, and stepped away. The goat came in only as far as the bowl and drank slowly. It did not touch the plants, as if it knew the rule too. Noor's flower moved as she laughed with relief.",
    "The neighbour appeared at the gate, saying sorry. Noor told him the water was already poured. She said the goat had been well behaved. He said thank you and led the animal away on a loose rope, without a yank. Then he went back to the shade of his own house. Noor washed the bowl and left it upside down to dry. It was ready for another thirst that was not a test. She did not shut the gate in anger. She left it resting against the wall.",
    "At night she told her grandmother the episode. Her grandmother recognised the goat from the tale in the real goat. She did not need a longer ending. Noor did not boast of being the only fair person in the neighbourhood. She said she had nearly closed the gate, and that the bowl had been the second idea. Her grandmother served water to the people at the table first. Noor saw the same gesture at the size of a house. The thobe had a drip on the tatreez sleeve, and she wiped it with her finger.",
    "If you are about to close a gate on an animal that is thirsty, stop first. See whether a bowl settles the visit. Noor filled the water for the neighbour's goat instead of leaving it outside. She remembered the goat that would not enter where they did not share. You can look after your plants and still offer a little of the well. A house gets cleverer when the door is not a hurry. A bowl on the ground says more than a latch.",
  ],
  "ps:2": [
    "The newly baked bread came with zaatar, the mixed thyme that smells of morning. Noor wanted the whole plate to herself on the kitchen step. She wore her thobe with tatreez, and the flower in her hair was still damp from washing her face. Her cousin stood at the door, just arrived from Jerusalem. She had come from the old houses and the market, her basket empty of simple stories. Her grandmother put the plate in the middle, not in front of one chair. Noor pulled it towards herself anyway, a hand's width. Her cousin did not pull back. She waited.",
    "Noor looked at the green zaatar on the oil. She remembered that the thyme in the bread is not planted for one mouth only. She broke off a piece and folded it in half with the filling inside. She held the fold out to her cousin. 'This is yours,' she said, and kept another, smaller than her first wish. Her cousin ate slowly and talked about the market, the baskets and the voices. She was in no hurry to finish the bread. Her grandmother cut one more fold for each of them. Noor's flower caught a crumb.",
    "They ate until they were comfortable, not until they reached some hidden bottom of the plate. A piece was still left for her grandfather when he came back from the shop. Noor covered it with a clean cloth and left it in sight. Her cousin helped wash her hands in a basin. The water turned green from a zaatar leaf that had fallen in, and nobody scolded her. The Bethlehem kitchen, at that hour, was bread, thyme and two chairs turned towards each other.",
    "Later Noor took a wrapped fold to the neighbour. The plate had shown the way to the door. The neighbour said 'Marhaba' and shared a little new oil in a small glass, for the next day's bread. Noor came back holding the glass with both hands, without spilling on the tatreez. Her grandmother praised the fold, not its size. The thobe smelled of zaatar. That was a good way to bring the market home without taking anything from the stalls.",
    "If you want the whole plate of bread with zaatar, make a fold first for whoever is at the door. Noor shared the thyme with her cousin, and the plate stayed in the middle. It was enough for the house and a parcel for the neighbour. You can love the smell and still not pull the plate to your own side. Bread folds. So does the morning, when there are two chairs and a little oil arriving yet.",
  ],
  "ps:3": [
    "A hoopoe landed on the wall between two houses. Its crest stood up like a small crown. It seemed to carry messages just by being there in the middle. Her grandmother said that, in one tale, the hoopoe carries a short sentence from one courtyard to the other. It comes back without being held. Noor, in her thobe with tatreez and a flower in her hair, wanted to hold the bird. She wanted it to take a real note to her cousin on the other side of the wall. Her hand went up, and the hoopoe tilted its head.",
    "She did not pull the bird and did not touch its feathers. She did not offer it the note in its beak as if it were a game. She sat down and wrote two kind lines on a small piece of paper. One hello, a wish that her cousin would come for a snack, and her name at the end. She folded the paper and went to the other house's door herself. The hoopoe stayed on the wall, free, smoothing its crest. Noor's flower went with her step. Her thobe brushed the wall without pulling a leaf from the vine.",
    "Her cousin read the note out loud and laughed at the round handwriting. She came for the snack without needing a bird postman. Noor said she had nearly pulled the hoopoe. Her cousin was glad the crest had stayed in place. Outside, the bird took off when it wanted, over the old houses, and nobody ran after it. The two of them ate bread and looked at the empty wall, now only a wall. They asked nothing of it. Noor's tatreez had a pencil mark, and she brushed it away.",
    "When her grandmother heard, she said the hoopoe tale teaches the short message. It does not teach the right to grab whoever has wings. Noor kept the answered note inside her notebook, with her cousin's drawing. She did not go back to the wall to tempt the bird with crumbs in a closed hand. If the hoopoe returned, it would be a visitor, not an employee. The afternoon in Jaffa could wait. That day the message had gone between two neighbouring doors, and that was enough. The flower went into a glass, straight as a crest.",
    "If you want to send a message, write a kind note and take it yourself. Do not pull a bird to do the walking. Noor left the hoopoe on the wall, its crest whole. Her cousin came for the snack because the words arrived in a person's hand. You can like a beautiful animal and still not turn it into a tool. The tale fits in a short sentence. The wings stay with the one born with them.",
  ],
  "ps:4": [
    "In the Jaffa market, among boxes of oranges, her grandmother bought just one. It was large and fragrant, and she said it was enough for two. Noor, in her thobe with tatreez and a flower in her hair, held the orange against her chest. It was as if the smell could escape if it were not all hers. The seller smiled and did not offer a second one. The bill was right, and the bag did not ask for more weight. Her grandmother took Noor into the shade of a low wall, away from the hurry of the stalls. 'Now we split it,' she said.",
    "Noor wanted to twist it on her own. She wanted to keep the bigger half without anyone seeing. Her grandmother did not stop her with a scolding. She guided her fingers slowly, until the peel gave way in two almost equal faces. The juice dripped on to a paper and not on to the tatreez. The flower in her hair caught a drop, and Noor laughed. Each of them had a half, and Noor's was not a prize: it was the measure. They ate there, watching the old port and the people with baskets.",
    "The sweet juice tasted of market and of home at the same time. Noor did not ask for her grandmother's peel to 'suck a bit more'. She folded the peels in the paper and took them to the basket, instead of leaving them on the wall. Her grandmother wiped her chin with a handkerchief. She did not turn the orange into a long lesson. The gesture of the joined hands splitting it was enough. Noor kept a pip in the handkerchief by mistake and took it out before leaving. She did not want to carry a seed without earth.",
    "On the way back she told her cousin about the Jaffa orange. One orange had been enough for two mouths. She said her grandmother's hands had helped to get the halves right. Her cousin wanted to see whether juice was still in the air. They laughed because the smell was still on the thobe. They did not go back to the market to ask for another 'to keep it whole'. There was bread at home, and the orange had already made its day. The flower, sticky from one drop, was washed and returned to her hair.",
    "If you have only one orange and someone beside you, split it with help. Keep the fair half, not the whole fruit hidden. Noor, in the Jaffa market, let her grandmother's hands guide hers. The juice reached both of them without a second purchase. You can want the whole smell and still open the peel in half. An orange split calmly is bigger than an orange shut against your chest. The market goes on, and the shade of the low wall is enough for those who share.",
  ],
  "sy:0": [
    "In Hama, the huge waterwheels groaned with a voice of wood. They groaned as the river passed through them, and the water went on to the gardens. Each garden took its turn. Rima wore the embroidered Syrian thobe with a flower in her hair. Her mother said 'Marhaba' to a neighbour waiting her turn by the sluice. Rima looked at her own garden, still dry at the edge, and at the neighbour's garden, already darkening with water. She wanted to open his sluice a little more, so their own row could drink at once. Her mother rested a hand on her arm.",
    "'The wheel sings and the water goes by turns,' said her mother. 'If you open what is not yours, the song loses its beat.' Rima moved away from the neighbour's sluice. She stood listening to the wooden groaning. It was not a complaint: it was work. The neighbour waved her thanks, without a speech, and her water ran straight between the cabbages. Rima sat on a stone in the shade and counted the turns of the wheel. She did this so the wait would not feel empty. The flower in her hair moved with the breeze that pushed the river.",
    "When their own garden's turn came, her mother opened their sluice, only theirs. The water came in gently, without stealing anyone's place in the queue. Rima helped guide a small channel with her toy wooden hoe. She did not put it in someone else's sluice. Their cabbages began to shine, and the neighbour's kept their own shine. Both were fair. Nobody ran to close what the other had opened. Below, the wheel groaned again, content with the whole river.",
    "In the afternoon Rima offered the neighbour a sprig of mint from the watered edge. She did not do it as payment. It was a continuation of a turn well done, and the neighbour gave her two plums. The two of them stood listening to the waterwheels for a moment, with hands free of latches. At home Rima told her grandmother about the wish to open what was not theirs. It had lasted a short while. She said the wait had lasted exactly right, while her grandmother poured fresh water. The embroidered thobe had a drop of river on its hem.",
    "If you are waiting for your turn of water, do not open the neighbour's sluice. The queue should not move at your hurry. Rima, in Hama, let the waterwheels sing and the water go to the gardens by turns. Their own garden drank when its hour came. You can be thirsty to see your green darken and still keep the river's beat. The wood groans because it works, not because someone is pushing it. Your sluice comes when it is yours.",
  ],
  "sy:1": [
    "In Damascus, the neighbour's jasmine hung over the courtyard wall. It was white and so fragrant that Rima stopped in the middle of a step. She wore her embroidered thobe and a flower in her hair. It looked like a poor cousin of that waterfall. Her mother said 'Marhaba' to the neighbour, who was watering on his own side. Rima brought her nose to the flowers and closed her eyes. She wanted to break off a sprig and put it in a glass in her room. Her fingers were already choosing a thin, loaded stem. Her mother coughed lightly, as a warning.",
    "'You smell from our side, and you pick from the side of the person who planted,' said her mother. Rima put her hand down without taking even a petal. She stayed there, breathing the jasmine. The smell was like a visit that is not kept in your pocket. From the other side the neighbour himself offered two flowers already fallen on his ground. Her mother thanked him and accepted only those, which the plant had let go. Rima put them in a glass. She was happy with the difference between falling and being picked. Her own hair flower stayed where it was.",
    "During the afternoon Rima passed the wall three times. Three times she only smelled, training her hand to stay by her skirt. She did not climb the wall and did not pull a sprig to her side. She did not hide a flower in her thobe sleeve. When the breeze grew, more jasmine fell on the neighbour's side. He laughed and said his ground was rich. Rima laughed too, without asking for the riches. Their courtyard kept the borrowed smell, which is the best kind, because it comes back.",
    "At night the two flowers in the glass still gave a little of themselves. Rima did not go to fetch more to 'renew the bunch'. She told her grandmother that the Damascus wall had a jasmine you visit with your nose. Her grandmother smelled the glass and said two fallen flowers are enough for one room. Rima slept with the window ajar, and the smell came in by itself. No fingernail helped it. In the morning the flowers were wilted and honest. She laid them on the earth of a pot, not in the street bin.",
    "If you see jasmine falling from the neighbour's wall, smell it and do not pick it. Take only what he gives you from what it has already let go. Rima, in Damascus, left the whole sprig and kept two fallen flowers in a glass. The courtyard went on smelling without one branch fewer. You can want all that white in your room and still leave the plant on the other side. The smell crosses the wall by itself. The hand does not need to help with a tug.",
  ],
  "sy:2": [
    "In the Aleppo market, a stubborn donkey stopped in the middle of the lane. Its owner pushed at its back with no result, sweating and speaking low. Rima, in her embroidered thobe with a flower in her hair, saw something about the pushing. Extra force only made the donkey dig in its hooves. The owner stopped, laughed at himself and offered water in a basin. The donkey drank, shook its ears and walked, as if the lane were a path again. Rima's mother said 'Marhaba' to him and moved on, without interfering. Rima had been holding her cousin's sleeve since the last stall.",
    "Her cousin wanted to look at the pistachios calmly. Rima was pulling her towards the ribbon stall, her hand closed on the fabric of the sleeve. She remembered the donkey and the useless pushing. She let go of the sleeve and said that water had done what force could not. She stood beside her cousin instead of dragging her along. Her cousin smiled and looked at the ribbons for a moment, on her own account. Then she went to the pistachios without being towed. Rima walked alongside, her hands visible and empty. The flower in her hair stopped swinging to the side.",
    "They bought a small handful together, half each. Nobody was left with a crumpled sleeve from all the pulling. Rima said sorry in a clear voice, and her cousin accepted without listing complaints. Further on they saw the donkey, now walking with its owner. The empty basin hung down, and the two were in agreement. Rima did not laugh at the animal. She waved from a distance, grateful for a lesson that had not been a shout. The embroidered thobe went on lightly between the busy stalls, full of voices and the smell of bread.",
    "At home Rima told her grandmother about the donkey. She showed that she knew how to let go of a sleeve, her cousin's and not a fruit's. Her grandmother gave her water without being asked. Rima laughed, because people walk better after a glass too. She did not pull her cousin again that week, not even for the good hurry of a game. They agreed on a place and went there. In her memory the market kept the donkey drinking and the lane opening without a push.",
    "If you are pulling someone by the sleeve to go faster, let go. Offer calm, like someone offering water. Rima saw the Aleppo market donkey walk after drinking, not after being pushed. She let her cousin choose her own pace. You can be in a hurry and still take your hand off other people's cloth. The lane fits two bodies side by side. Too much force only digs in the hooves, even when the hooves are a child's wish.",
  ],
  "sy:3": [
    "At home in Aleppo, her grandmother showed her a new bar of soap made from olive oil. She said that soap waits many months until it is ready. Rima wore her embroidered thobe and a flower in her hair. She wanted to take the soap to the basin that very day. It smelled good and already looked finished. Her grandmother did not hide it away strictly. She explained that the hurry would leave the soap soft and the work spoiled. She put the new bar on a high shelf, in the shade, with the month's name written on a paper. Rima looked up at it, her hands full of wanting.",
    "'For today there is this one,' said her grandmother. She gave her a bar already ready. It was darker and firm, and it had done its waiting at some other time. Rima washed her hands with it, slowly, and the foam came out right. It did not turn the bar into a mush. The new one stayed on the shelf, untouched, doing its months like someone doing a useful sleep. Rima's hair flower caught a bubble, and she laughed and wiped it on the towel. The smell from the shelf came down by itself.",
    "In the days that followed Rima looked at the paper with the month on it. She did not move the date forward with her fingernail. She used the ready bar to its honest end. When it grew small her grandmother gave her another ripe one, never the one on the high shelf. Her thobe was washed with that olive soap, and the embroidery stayed soft. It was not rough from hurried experience. Rima told her cousin the difference between smelling and being ready. Her cousin wanted to touch the new bar. Waiting is part of the recipe.",
    "A long time later, her grandmother brought the bar down and put it in the basin. Rima recognised the smell and the firmness too. She did not say, 'I told you it was ready on the first day.' She washed her hands again and said thank you to the months. That made her grandmother laugh. The paper with the month went into her notebook, as a reminder of a wait that had been worth it. The flower that day was another one, but the gesture was the same. The house smelled of good oil and of patience.",
    "If you see something newly made that still needs to wait, do not use it on the first day. It might smell good, but that is not the same as ready. Rima, in Aleppo, washed her hands with the ready bar. The new one rested on the shelf for its months. You can want to try it and still let time finish the recipe. Olive oil soap is not rushed. The right foam is the one that comes on the day the bar knows how to be firm.",
  ],
  "sy:4": [
    "In the market, the pistachio seller filled the plate up to the painted line on the tin. Not one grain above, for favour, and not one below, for hurry. Rima, in her embroidered thobe with a flower in her hair, saw the pile exactly on the line. She wanted to say 'just a little more'. She had a smile that sometimes makes grown-ups give in. Her mother had given the right money for that line. The seller waited politely, without pushing and without tricking. The line was a sentence without words. Rima bit her tongue on the easy sentence and looked at her mother.",
    "Her mother did not wag a finger. She only touched the painted line, like someone reading it. Rima breathed and said it was right as it was. The seller smiled, poured the pistachios into the bag and returned the plate to the tin. The line stayed visible for the next customer. There was no hidden favour and no hidden shortage. Rima held the bag with both hands and felt the fair weight. It was neither too little nor too much. The flower in her hair stayed still, as if it had accepted the measure too.",
    "They sat on a bench at the edge of the busy stalls. Rima ate slowly and did not ask for a little more again with her mouth already full. She offered a handful to her mother, and another was kept for her grandmother, wrapped separately. The bag emptied in three fair parts, and nobody said the line had been unfair. A boy nearby asked his father for 'more', and Rima did not laugh at him. She remembered the sentence that had been in her own mouth. The boy's father pointed at the line.",
    "At home her grandmother praised the weight, not the little heap that could have come extra. Rima told the truth, that she had wanted to ask and had not. In that kitchen, that was worth more than a heavier bag. They ate the pistachios after the bread, as a market dessert, and kept the shells in a paper. The embroidered thobe smelled a little of salt and of a small celebration. The flower went into the glass. The line on the tin, back at the market, went on working without them.",
    "If you see a plate filled to the fair line, do not ask for 'just a little more'. Rima, in front of the pistachios, let the right weight stay right. The seller filled the tin as always, without favour and without hurry. You can want one more grain and still recognise the painted line. What is fair is not too little: it is the size where nobody loses. An honest bag is enough for your mother, your grandmother and you.",
  ],
};
export default PART;
